Now I understand the setup. The user wants htop on tty1 on hp348 without needing to log in. Let me plan the approach.

Since `server.nix` is shared across all OCI hosts, I should put the hp348-specific bits in `hosts/hp348/configuration.nix`.

The approach:
1. Auto-login `duck` on tty1 — this uses `services.getty.autologinUser`
2. When `duck` logs in on tty1, automatically run `htop` — this can be done via `programs.bash.shellInit` or `users.users.duck.shell` pointing to a custom script

Options:

**Option A: Auto-login + bash shellInit (simplest)**

```nix
# In hp348/configuration.nix
services.getty.autologinUser = "duck";

programs.bash.shellInit = ''
  if [ "$(tty)" = "/dev/tty1" ]; then
    exec htop
  fi
'';
```

Pros: Simple, uses existing NixOS options
Cons: `exec htop` means exiting htop logs you out; shellInit affects all shells, not just tty1

Actually, `shellInit` would apply to all bash invocations. We should use `interactiveShellInit` or check `$(tty)`. The approach above checks tty so it only affects tty1.

But wait — `shellInit` runs in every bash instance (including scripts). Better to use `interactiveShellInit` since htop needs an interactive terminal anyway. But the auto-login getty starts an interactive login shell, so `shellInit` should work there.

Actually, for NixOS, `programs.bash.shellInit` is added to `/etc/bashrc` (for interactive shells) and also to `.bashrc` equivalent. Let me think...

In NixOS:
- `programs.bash.shellInit` → goes into `/etc/bashrc` (sourced by interactive non-login shells)
- `programs.bash.loginShellInit` → goes into `/etc/profile` (sourced by login shells)
- `programs.bash.interactiveShellInit` → goes into `/etc/bashrc` (sourced by interactive shells)

Getty with auto-login typically starts a login shell, so `loginShellInit` would be the right place. But the `exec htop` approach means the user can't get a shell on tty1 without SSH.

**Option B: systemd service with htop on tty1 (no login at all)**

```nix
systemd.services.htop-console = {
  enable = true;
  description = "htop system monitor on tty1";
  after = [ "systemd-user-sessions.service" ];
  wants = [ "systemd-user-sessions.service" ];
  serviceConfig = {
    Type = "simple";
    ExecStart = "${pkgs.htop}/bin/htop";
    Restart = "always";
    RestartSec = 10;
    TTYPath = "/dev/tty1";
    StandardInput = "tty";
    StandardOutput = "tty";
    TTYReset = true;
    TTYVHangup = true;
    TTYVTDisallocate = true;
  };
  wantedBy = [ "multi-user.target" ];
};
```

This runs htop directly on tty1 without any login. When you switch to tty1, htop is there. The service restarts if htop exits.

I think Option B is what the user is asking for: "without logging in". They want a dashboard visible on the physical console without any interaction.

Let me present both options to the user.

